INT. NEONATAL ICU - NURSES' STATION - 3 A.M.

Low lights. Monitors blink like fireflies. Incubators glow along the far wall.

DR. SAOIRSE ACHEBE-LINDGREN, 40s, scrubs rumpled, drinks cold tea. NURSE PERPETUA OKONKWO-HART, 50s, charts at the desk. RESIDENT DR. ELIAS FAIRWEATHER-NNAMDI, 20s, rubs his eyes. And the father, HOLLIS BRZEZINSKI-OSEI, 30s, in a jacket over pyjamas, stands with a paper cup he has not touched.

SAOIRSE
Mr. Brzezinski-Osei, sit down.

HOLLIS
I'd rather stand.

SAOIRSE
Please.

HOLLIS
Is she worse?

SAOIRSE
She's the same.

HOLLIS
The same.

PERPETUA
Her numbers are stable.

ELIAS
Her oxygen sats are ninety-four.

HOLLIS
Is that good?

SAOIRSE
It's better than yesterday.

HOLLIS
But it's not good.

ELIAS
It's within range.

HOLLIS
Whose range?

SAOIRSE
Ours.

PERPETUA
Mr. Brzezinski-Osei, she is fighting.

HOLLIS
She weighs less than a bag of sugar.

SAOIRSE
She weighs one kilo, four hundred.

HOLLIS
Everyone keeps telling me the number.

ELIAS
It's what we have.

HOLLIS
I have a name for her.

SAOIRSE
We know. Matilda.

HOLLIS
She doesn't know it.

PERPETUA
She does. I say it every shift.

HOLLIS
You say it?

PERPETUA
Every shift, Mr. Brzezinski-Osei.

Hollis puts down the cup. Presses his palms to his eyes.

HOLLIS
Can I hold her?

SAOIRSE
Not tonight.

HOLLIS
When?

SAOIRSE
When her numbers hold for twenty-four hours.

HOLLIS
And if they don't?

ELIAS
They will.

HOLLIS
You don't know that.

ELIAS
No. I don't.

Silence. A monitor chirps. Perpetua looks up, checks the bay, nods.

PERPETUA
She's fine. A feeding pump.

Hollis laughs, shaky. Sits. Finally.

HOLLIS
Tell me about the pump.

SAOIRSE
It's a pump.

HOLLIS
Tell me anyway.

Saoirse sits across from him. Takes his hand. Begins to explain.
